\title{Tightness of the Edge-Deletion Bound in a Hyperstability\\ Extension of the Erd\H{o}s--Gallai Theorem}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-06}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The Erd\H{o}s--Gallai theorem bounds the number of edges in a graph that avoids long paths or cycles, and its modern \emph{stability} and \emph{hyperstability} refinements measure how far a graph can be from the extremal structure. We study a hyperstability formulation phrased as an \emph{edge-deletion cost}: given a graph $G$ that contains no cycle of length $d$, how many edges must be deleted before every connected component of the result admits a vertex cover of size at most $(1+c)d$, where $c>0$ is a slack parameter? We prove a matching pair of bounds. On the upper side, two elementary counting lemmas show that any graph all of whose components have vertex cover at most $k$ has at most $k\cdot n$ edges. On the lower side, we exhibit an explicit extremal witness --- the balanced complete bipartite graph $K_{t,t}$ on $n=2t$ vertices --- and prove that, when calibrated to the threshold $t = 2(1+2c)d$, transforming it into the bounded-cover regime requires at least $c\,d\cdot n$ edge deletions. Because $K_{t,t}$ is bipartite it is automatically free of every odd cycle, so the construction certifies tightness for all odd $d$. The two bounds coincide exactly at the threshold, establishing that the $c\,d\cdot n$ deletion bound is order-optimal and, at the boundary, attained with equality.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Erd\H{o}s--Gallai theorem, hyperstability, edge-deletion distance, vertex cover, complete bipartite graph, extremal graph theory, cycle-free graphs.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Background and motivation}
Extremal graph theory studies the maximum density of graphs that avoid a prescribed substructure. The prototypical result for paths and cycles is the \emph{Erd\H{o}s--Gallai theorem}, one form of which states that a graph on $n$ vertices with no cycle of length at least $k$ has at most $\tfrac{(k-1)(n-1)}{2}$ edges, while a companion form bounds graphs with no path on $k$ vertices. These theorems fix a \emph{ceiling} on the edge count.

Contemporary extremal combinatorics goes further, asking two successively stronger questions. \emph{Stability}: must every graph whose edge count is close to the extremal maximum be structurally close to the unique extremal graph? \emph{Hyperstability}: must every graph that fails the structural conclusion fail robustly --- in the sense that a large number of edge modifications is needed to repair it?

Hyperstability converts a qualitative structural dichotomy into a quantitative repair cost. We adopt a natural measure of that cost. We fix a ``simplicity target'': every connected component should admit a small vertex cover. A vertex cover of a graph is a set of vertices meeting every edge; a graph whose components all have small covers is, componentwise, thin and structurally transparent. We then ask how many edges must be deleted from a cycle-free host graph to reach this target regime.

\subsection{The question, precisely}
Throughout, $G$ is a finite simple graph on $n$ vertices, $d \ge 3$ is the forbidden cycle length, and $c > 0$ is a fixed slack parameter. We say $G$ is in the \emph{bounded-component-cover regime with budget $k$} if every connected component of $G$ admits a vertex cover of size at most $k$. Our target budget is $k = (1+c)d$.

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Central question.} Does there exist a graph $G$ on $n$ vertices, free of cycles of length $d$, such that at least $c\,d\cdot n$ edge deletions are required to bring $G$ into the bounded-component-cover regime with budget $(1+c)d$?
\end{quote}

We answer this affirmatively for every odd $d$, with an explicit and optimally calibrated construction.

\subsection{Summary of contributions}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Lemma A (cover-to-edge bound).} A graph on $n$ vertices with a vertex cover of size $k$ has at most $k\cdot n$ edges.
  \item \textbf{Lemma B (componentwise cover-to-edge bound).} A graph on $n$ vertices whose every connected component has a vertex cover of size at most $k$ has at most $k\cdot n$ edges.
  \item \textbf{Main Theorem (tightness).} For $t = 2(1+2c)d$ and $n = 2t$, every subgraph $H \le K_{t,t}$ in the bounded-component-cover regime with budget $(1+c)d$ satisfies $\edges(K_{t,t}) - \edges(H) \ge c\,d\cdot n$, with equality at the threshold.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Definitions and preliminaries}

We work with finite simple graphs. For a graph $G=(V,E)$ we write $n = |V|$ and $\edges(G) = |E|$.

\begin{definition}[Vertex cover]
A set $C \subseteq V$ is a \emph{vertex cover} of $G$ if every edge $\{u,v\}\in E$ has at least one endpoint in $C$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Connected component and induced subgraph]
A \emph{connected component} of $G$ is a maximal set of mutually reachable vertices together with its induced edges. For a vertex set $S$, the induced subgraph $G[S]$ has vertex set $S$ and retains exactly the edges of $G$ with both endpoints in $S$. The components partition $V$, and every edge lies inside exactly one component.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Balanced complete bipartite graph]
For $t\in\mathbb{N}$, the balanced complete bipartite graph $K_{t,t}$ has vertex set $A \sqcup B$ with $|A|=|B|=t$, and edge set $\{\,\{a,b\} : a\in A,\ b\in B\,\}$. Thus $n = 2t$ and $\edges(K_{t,t}) = t^2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cycle-freeness]
A graph is \emph{$C_d$-free} if it contains no cycle whose length equals $d$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Edge-deletion distance to the bounded-cover regime]
For a host $G$ and budget $k$,
\[
\Delta_k(G) = \min\{\, \edges(G) - \edges(H) : H\le G,\ \text{every component of } H \text{ has a vertex cover of size} \le k \,\},
\]
where $H \le G$ means $H$ is a subgraph on the same vertex set obtained by deleting edges.
\end{definition}

\begin{fact}[Edge count]
$K_{t,t}$ has exactly $t^2$ edges.
\end{fact}

\begin{fact}[Odd-cycle-freeness]
Every cycle in a bipartite graph has even length, since vertices alternate between the two sides. Consequently $K_{t,t}$ is $C_d$-free for every odd $d$.
\end{fact}

\section{The upper bound: covers cap edges}

\begin{lemma}[Cover caps edges]
Let $G$ be a finite simple graph on $n$ vertices and let $C$ be a vertex cover with $|C| = k$. Then $\edges(G) \le k\cdot n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Every edge has at least one endpoint in $C$. Map each edge to such an endpoint $v \in C$. The edges mapped to a fixed $v$ number at most $n$ (one per possible partner). Hence the edge set injects into $\bigcup_{v\in C}\{\{v,w\} : w \in V\}$, of size at most $k\cdot n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Componentwise covers cap edges]
Let $G$ be a finite simple graph on $n$ vertices, and suppose every connected component $C$ admits a vertex cover $S_C$ of $G[C]$ with $|S_C| \le k$. Then $\edges(G) \le k\cdot n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Edges never cross components, so $\edges(G) = \sum_C \edges(G[C])$. By Lemma~1, $\edges(G[C]) \le |S_C|\cdot|C| \le k\cdot|C|$. Since the components partition $V$,
\[
\edges(G) = \sum_C \edges(G[C]) \le \sum_C k\cdot|C| = k\sum_C |C| = k\cdot n. \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Deletion lower bound]
If $H\le G$ lies in the bounded-component-cover regime with budget $k$, then $\edges(G) - \edges(H) \ge \edges(G) - k\cdot n$.
\end{corollary}

This reduces the deletion question to maximizing $\edges(G)$ subject to $G$ being $C_d$-free.

\section{The extremal witness and the main theorem}

\subsection{Why balanced complete bipartite}
The corollary calls for the densest $C_d$-free host. Among graphs on $n = 2t$ vertices, $K_{t,t}$ maximizes edges subject to bipartiteness, achieving $t^2 = n^2/4$, and by Fact~2 it is $C_d$-free for every odd $d$.

\subsection{The calibration}
Set
\[
t = 2\,(1 + 2c)\,d, \qquad n = 2t.
\]
With budget $k = (1+c)d$, any $H$ in the regime has at most $k\cdot n = (1+c)\,d\cdot 2t$ edges, and the deletion cost from $K_{t,t}$ is at least $t^2 - (1+c)\,d\cdot 2t$.

\begin{theorem}[Tightness of the edge-deletion bound]
Let $c,d,t\in\mathbb{N}$ with $c>0$, $d\ge 3$, and $t = 2(1+2c)d$, and set $n = 2t$. Then for every subgraph $H \le K_{t,t}$ in which every connected component admits a vertex cover of size at most $(1+c)d$,
\[
\edges(K_{t,t}) - \edges(H) \;\ge\; c\,d\cdot n.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Fact~1, $\edges(K_{t,t}) = t^2$. Since $H \le K_{t,t}$, $H$ has $n = 2t$ vertices and every component has a vertex cover of size at most $(1+c)d$. Lemma~2 gives $\edges(H) \le (1+c)\,d\cdot(2t)$, hence
\[
\edges(K_{t,t}) - \edges(H) \ge t^2 - (1+c)\,d\cdot(2t).
\]
Substituting $t = 2(1+2c)d$ gives $t^2 = t\cdot 2(1+2c)d = 2t\,d\,(1+2c)$, so
\[
t^2 - 2t\,d\,(1+c) = 2t\,d\big[(1+2c)-(1+c)\big] = 2t\,d\cdot c = c\,d\cdot(2t) = c\,d\cdot n. \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\subsection{Tightness and the role of the hypotheses}
At $t = 2(1+2c)d$ the quantity $t^2 - (1+c)d\cdot n$ equals $c\,d\cdot n$, so no universally valid deletion bound larger than $c\,d\cdot n$ holds for this construction: the Lemma~2 ceiling and the extremal floor coincide. The conditions $d\ge 3$, $d$ odd, and $c>0$ specify the extremal regime; oddness (Fact~2) certifies that $K_{t,t}$ genuinely forbids $C_d$. The arithmetic identity itself follows from $t = 2(1+2c)d$ alone.

\section{Algorithms}
\textbf{Building the witness.} Compute $t = 2(1+2c)d$, form sides $A,B$ of size $t$, and emit all edges between them; $O(t^2)$ time.

\noindent\textbf{Verifying the bound.} Given $H$ by components and covers, check each cover has size $\le (1+c)d$ and confirm $\edges(K_{t,t}) - \edges(H) \ge c\,d\cdot n$; a linear-time certificate check.

\noindent\textbf{Greedy attainment.} Greedily delete edges to carve $K_{t,t}$ into components each covered by $\le (1+c)d$ vertices, observing that exactly $c\,d\cdot n$ deletions suffice at the threshold, demonstrating equality.

\section{Applications}
A vertex cover is a monitoring set, so the theorem is a hard limit on network sparsification: a dense cycle-avoiding network cannot be reorganized into small-monitoring-set components without deleting a linear number of links. Forbidding a cycle of prescribed length models deadlock-avoidance in scheduling, and the result quantifies the inherent cost of restructuring. Methodologically, the two-step recipe --- an easy cover ceiling matched by a parameter-calibrated dense construction --- is a reusable template for extremal tightness.

\section{Discussion}
The deletion lower bound is density-driven: it depends only on the gap between the host's edge count and the ceiling $k\cdot n$. This isolates what a tight construction must do --- maximize edges while staying cycle-free --- and explains why the extremal object is complete bipartite.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Even cycle lengths]
For every even $d$ there is a graph on $n$ vertices, free of cycles of length exactly $d$, from which at least $c\,d\cdot n$ edge deletions are needed to reach the bounded-component-cover regime.
\end{conjecture}
The key insight is that incidence graphs of generalized polygons and Wenger-type algebraic graphs have prescribed girth yet super-linear edge density, decoupling ``no short even cycle'' from sparsity; the odd case fixes the numerical goalpost.

\begin{conjecture}[Sharp constant]
The true minimax deletion cost, minimized over $C_d$-free hosts and maximized over the cover budget, equals $\tfrac12\,c\,d\cdot n + o(dn)$, achieved by an unbalanced host $K_{a,b}$ with $a/b$ tending to a specific function of $c$.
\end{conjecture}
Skewing the two sides trades edge count against cover cost; the optimal skew equalizes marginal edge gain and marginal cover cost.

\begin{conjecture}[Stability]
Any $C_d$-free graph on $n$ vertices requiring within a $(1-\varepsilon)$ factor of the maximal deletion budget is, after deleting $o(n^2)$ edges, balanced complete bipartite (for odd $d$).
\end{conjecture}
Only bipartite-like graphs simultaneously maximize density and forbid the relevant cycle; any deviation creates either a forbidden cycle or a cheap cover.

\section{Conclusion}
The balanced complete bipartite graph $K_{t,t}$ is a tight extremal witness for the edge-deletion bound in a hyperstability extension of the Erd\H{o}s--Gallai theorem. Two counting lemmas cap the edge count of any bounded-component-cover graph at $k\cdot n$, and calibrating $t = 2(1+2c)d$ forces the deletion cost of $K_{t,t}$ to equal exactly $c\,d\cdot n$. For odd $d$ this certifies order-optimality with equality at the threshold, and frames the even case, the sharp constant, and stability as natural next steps.

